    ["developer_files",
    ".VIC",
    "ViziGen Code Import Mappings File",
    "3.0",
    "Contains import mappings used by ViziGen, a software development program for generating source code files for any programming language; controls how information from a UML modeling tool is imported into ViziGen. Windows ViziGen. Image created in the VICAR (Video Image Access and Retrieval) format, which was originally developed for planetary mission imaging data; contains an ASCII text header and one or more images saved as records of binary data; most commonly known for storing captured images from planetary mission systems. VICAR images were designed for the Jet Propulsion Laboratorys (JPL) unmanned planetary spacecraft. They were used during the 1970s and 1980s for the following missions: Mariner 9 to Mars, Mariner 10 to Mercury and Venus, Voyager to the outer planets. VICAR images are also used for cartography, biomedical, geology, and astronomy applications. Windows Newera Graphics Converter Pro. IMG2PNG XnViewMP. Mac XnViewMP. Linux XnViewMPFree.",
    "Windows ViziGen. Windows Newera Graphics Converter Pro. IMG2PNG XnViewMP. Mac XnViewMP. Linux XnViewMP.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".VSPSCC",
    "Visual Studio Project Source Control File",
    "3.0",
    "Source Code Control (SCC) file for projects created with Microsoft Visual Studio; contains metadata used for tracking revisions of Visual Studio software development projects; includes information such as the version number, file path, and the number of nested projects. Source code control files are used to prevent multiple users from editing the same file at the same time. They allow developers to check in and check out files from a single location, such as a network drive. VSPSCC files are typically saved along with a corresponding.VSSSCC file. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022.",
    "Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".LUCIDSNIPPET",
    "Lucid Snippet",
    "3.0",
    "A LUCIDSNIPPET file was created for use with Lucid, a JavaScript web animation building program. It contains a default or customized action that Lucid uses to add functions to a webpage. The default LUCIDSNIPPET files can be located by following these steps: Open the Finder window and right-click the Lucid application icon in the location you saved it (most likely under Applications). Select Show Package Contents → Contents → Resources → Objects, the files will be located in these folders, theyre organized the same way they appear on the object bar in the Lucid window. You can create a new LUCIDSNIPPET file by clicking on the Action (under ACTIONS in the Lucid window) you want to save and then clicking the Save Snippet clipboard icon in the top left. After you fill out the name and description, click Save to Snippets. The LUCIDSNIPPET file is now created and saved in your Snippets folder. googlemap.lucidsnippet - Google Map action that enables you to place a Google Map on your webpage; found in the Google folder. animate.lucidsnippet - Animate action that enables you to animate the alteration of a property; found in the jQuery folder. Mac The Escapers Lucid.",
    "Mac The Escapers Lucid.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".BBPROJECT",
    "BBEdit Project File",
    "3.0",
    "Project file created by BBEdit, a source code text editor for Mac; saved in an XML format and contains references to files included in the project; allows developers to group and organize source code for software projects. The BBPROJECT file format is an older format for BBEdit projects. The software now uses the.BBPROJECTD (BBEdit Project Document) format for projects. Mac Bare Bones BBEdit.",
    "Mac Bare Bones BBEdit.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".JSH",
    "Jsh Script",
    "3.0",
    "Script header file written for JAWS; a Windows screen reading application that provides text to speech functionality for Windows text; stores header information for a.JSS source file; used in the process of developing automated JAWS screen reading tasks. Windows Freedom Scientific JAWS. Script written for Jsh (Java shell), a shell program that allows Java commands and programs to be run; saves a series of commands that are in the same format as if you were to type them one by one at the Jsh command line. JSH files are used for writing convenience scripts for running common tasks. Windows Jsh. Mac Jsh. Linux JshFree.",
    "Windows Freedom Scientific JAWS. Windows Jsh. Mac Jsh. Linux Jsh.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".TEXTFACTORY",
    "BBEdit Text Factory",
    "3.0",
    "Developer file created by BBEdit, a Mac OS X source code editor; contains rules for automatic processing of text; may contain text replacements, sorting instructions, or other manipulations; used for saving and reusing text factory settings. Text factory files allow developers to perform batch updates of source code files. For example, a developer could use a TEXTFACTORY file to change all header information in project files. BBEdit can run text factories in the background so that development can continue while the program performs the updates. NOTE: TEXTFACTORY files are also used by Bare Bones Softwares Text Wrangler editor. Mac Bare Bones BBEdit.",
    "Mac Bare Bones BBEdit.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".DCUIL",
    "Delphi .NET Compiled Unit File",
    "3.0",
    "Developer file created by Delphi, an application used for building Windows programs; similar to a.DCU file (Delphis native Win32 compiled unit format) but is compiled for Microsofts.NET Framework; compiled from a.PAS file. Windows Embarcadero Delphi.",
    "Windows Embarcadero Delphi.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".PLE",
    "Messenger Plus! Live Encrypted Log File",
    "3.0",
    "Log file containing saved chat sessions from Windows Live Messenger with the Messenger Plus! Live extension; saves text from instant messaging sessions in an encrypted format that can only be opened with Messenger Plus! Live. Windows Patchou Messenger Plus! Live.",
    "Windows Patchou Messenger Plus! Live.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".TMLANGUAGE",
    "TextMate Language Grammar File",
    "3.0",
    "Language grammar file used by TextMate, a Mac OS X source code editor; contains a specification for the syntax of a programming language; used for adding syntax highlighting support for additional languages. TMLANGUAGE files are saved using XML formatting but may be encoded with a binary representation of the XML data. NOTE: TextMate language grammar files are often saved as binary Property List (.PLIST) files. For example, some of the.TMBUNDLE files included with the TextMate installation include a PLIST file in their /​Syntaxes/​ directory. Mac MacroMates TextMateFree.",
    "Mac MacroMates TextMate.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".REB",
    "REBOL Script File",
    "3.0",
    "Script that contains code written in the REBOL (Relative Expression Based Object Language) programming language; can be compiled into an executable program using a REBOL compiler. NOTE: The default extension for REBOL scripts is.R. The .reb extension may be used if the .r extension is already used by another application. Windows REBOL/View REBOL/Core REBOL/SDK Mac REBOL/View REBOL/Core REBOL/SDK Linux REBOL/View REBOL/Core REBOL/SDK. Data file created by Reimbursement Tracker, a program used to track and submit reimbursements; contains a collection of entries, each of which include the reimbursement date, merchant store, description, and amount. REB files are used for storing submitting reimbursement forms, as well as printing them using standard or thermal printers. Windows Reimbursement TrackerFree.",
    "Windows REBOL/View REBOL/Core REBOL/SDK. Mac REBOL/View REBOL/Core REBOL/SDK. Linux REBOL/View REBOL/Core REBOL/SDK. Windows Reimbursement Tracker.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".RESJSON",
    "Windows JavaScript Resources File",
    "3.0",
    "Developer file used by Windows Metro style apps developed for Windows 8; saved in a JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) format and contains strings that are often used for localizing the apps user interface. Developers often create different RESJSON files (e.g., resources.resjson) in folders named /​en-US/​, /​fr-FR/​, /​ja-JP/​, etc. Each resources.resjson file has strings localized for the particular language folder. NOTE: Resources may also be saved using.RESW files if using the C# and C++ languages. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022. Any text editor.",
    "Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022. Any text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".CVSRC",
    "CVS Command File",
    "3.0",
    "File created for TortoiseCVS, a freely shared Concurrent Versions System (CVS) client for Microsoft Windows; contains default options that are added to cvs_commands within CVS instead of using other shell scripts. If you dont want to use the options in the file, use the -f global option, for e.g., cvs -f update. NOTE: The CVS keeps track of project progress and all its changes in a set of files. By doing this, CVS allows different users to collaborate on projects by creating working copies of the work then sending their completed work to the server. Other collaborators can then see their work and work off of those files. Windows TortoiseCVSFree.",
    "Windows TortoiseCVS.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".PKGUNDEF",
    "Visual Studio Shell File",
    "3.0",
    "File created by Microsoft Visual Studio, a Windows system SDK; contains metadata, much like the.PKGDEF file, but is used to exclude certain registry entries from an isolated shell application; can list multiple registries to exclude. When the isolated shell application is run a registry key is created for the application. The registrys appearances and behavior can be modified by PKGDEF files. The PKGUNDEF files are used to remove Visual Studio shell components from the application by removing specified keys and entries. To remove a command function in the isolated shell application, you would uncomment the line (example) [$RootKey$Packages{8D8529D3-625D-4496-8354-3DAD630ECC1B}] in the PKGUNDEF file. The location of PKGUNDEF files in, say Visual Studio 11.0, is C:/​Program Files (x86)/​Microsoft Visual Studio/​11.0/​Common7/​IDE/​. It will have the same name as the corresponding PKGDEF file. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022.",
    "Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".WS",
    "Whitespace Source Code",
    "3.0",
    "Executable script for Windows that can incorporate JScript and VBScript routines and may include XML elements; runs when opened if Windows Scripting Host (WSH) 2.0 or later is installed. NOTE: Do not open as an e-mail attachment, since WS files can contain viruses or other malicious programs. Windows Microsoft WScript Included with OS. Any text editor. A WS file is a game ROM file used by WonderSwan emulators, such as Oswan, OpenEmu, and Higan. It contains an exact copy of the game data dumped from the original WonderSwan game cartridge. WonderSwan emulators allow users to play WonderSwan games on a computer as if they were run on the original system. Game data from the original WonderSwan game cartridge is dumped into a WS file, which stores the game data in a format that can be played by a WonderSwan emulator. WonderSwan is a Japanese handheld video game console created by Bandai. It was released in 1999 as a competitor to Nintendos Game Boy Color. It was discontinued in 2003. WonderSwan game titles include Buffers Evolution, Beatmania, Final Lap 2000, Gunpey, Harobots, and Puzzle Bobble. Windows Oswan. Mednafen. Higan. Mac OpenEmu. OswanFree. File created by 1-2-3 Word Search Maker, a program used for making word search puzzles; stores a word list as well as the alphabetical grid containing the words to find; also stores the solution by highlighting the word list in the puzzle. When you have completed designing a word puzzle, you can print it with a standard printer. NOTE: WS files are saved in a simple text format and can be viewed with any text editor to browse the contents. Windows Variety Games 1-2-3 Word Search Maker. A WS file is a program written in the Whitespace programming language. It contains Whitespace source code, which consists of spaces, tabs, and linefeeds (whitespace characters). WS files also often contain comments written using letters, numbers, and other non-whitespace characters. WS file open in Microsoft Visual Studio Code Whitespace is a programming language developed by Edwin Brady and Chris Morris. The Whitespace interpreter ignores any non-whitespace characters, so Whitespace programs are written using only spaces, tabs, and linefeeds, which the interpreter reads as either commands or data. Commands are written using preset sequences of spaces, tabs, and linefeeds. Other data is written in binary, using spaces as 0s and tabs as 1s. While Whitespace is not used for serious development, some developers enjoy developing within the languages limitations and creating fun Whitespace programs. These developers may save their Whitespace programs as WS files. You can open and edit a WS file in any text or source code editor, such as Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Keep in mind that, because Whitespace programs are written using only whitespace characters, your WS file may appear empty. You can run the program a WS file contains using a Whitespace interpreter. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor Whitespace. Mac Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor Whitespace. Linux Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor WhitespaceFree.",
    "Windows Microsoft WScript Included with OS. Any text editor. Windows Oswan. Mednafen. Higan. Mac OpenEmu. Oswan. Windows Variety Games 1-2-3 Word Search Maker. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor Whitespace. Mac Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor Whitespace. Linux Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor Whitespace.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".WIXOBJ",
    "WiX Object File",
    "3.0",
    "Object file created by the WiX toolset, a Windows installer developing program; contains multiple sections that hold symbols and references to other symbols; comprised of <table>, <row>, and <field> elements holding data that is placed into the Windows Installer database; created when each WiX Source file is compiled. NOTE: WiX Source (.WXS) files are preprocessed and compiled into WIXOBJ files by the Candle tool in the WiX Toolset. Windows WiX ToolsetFree.",
    "Windows WiX Toolset.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".FBZ7",
    "Compressed FinalBuilder 7 Project",
    "3.0",
    "Project file created by FinalBuilder 7, a program used for building Windows software development projects into Windows applications; saves references to the assets required for building the program as well as relevant properties; used for constructing automated builds. FBZ7 files are the compressed version of.FBP7 project files. While both formats can be saved in FinalBuilder, the FBZ7 format is the default format. NOTE: FinalBuilder is included with some versions of Embarcadero Delphi. Windows VSoft Technologies FinalBuilder.",
    "Windows VSoft Technologies FinalBuilder.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".WIXOUT",
    "WiX XML Output File",
    "3.0",
    "Output file created by the WiX toolset, a Windows installer developing program; generated when several object files (.WIXOBJ) are linked; the XML form of the final output; not intended to be manually opened. Windows WiX ToolsetFree.",
    "Windows WiX Toolset.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".SCALE",
    "Scale Source Code",
    "3.0",
    "A SCALE file contains source code written in the Scale programming language. The code a SCALE file contains is meant to be compiled to C source code, using the source-to-source Scale compiler. SCALE file open in Microsoft Visual Studio Code The Scale programming language, developed by GitHub user StonkDragon, is inspired by Porth (.PORTH) and Lua (.LUA). It is a procedural, object-oriented, concatenative, and stack-oriented language. Developers save Scale source code in SCALE files. These files contain some number of Scale functions. There is no compiler that converts SCALE files to machine code. Instead, the Scale compiler converts SCALE files to C code, which can then be included in C programs. You can open and edit a SCALE file with Microsoft Visual Studio Code (multiplatform) or any other source code or text editor. You can use the Scale compiler (macOS, Linux) to compile a SCALE file to C code. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor Mac Scale Compiler. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor Linux Scale Compiler. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor.",
    "Windows Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor. Mac Scale Compiler. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor. Linux Scale Compiler. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Any text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".TLD",
    "Tag Library Descriptor File",
    "3.0",
    "Developer file used for Java Server Page (JSP) development projects; saved using XML formatting and contains descriptions of tags, which are used to markup JSP documents; describes the supported tags in a corresponding tag library. A JSP tag is similar to an HTML tag. For example, a custom tag <loadData> could be used for loading data into a JSP Web page. The custom tag library would have a class handler for this type of tag and the TLD file would describe the tag. Tag libraries and tag descriptors are included with Java-based Web applications. Often the TLD file is packaged as part of a.JAR file that is located in the /​WEB-INF/​lib/​ directory. Windows Eclipse IDE for Java Developers. Apache NetBeans. Microsoft XML Notepad. Other text editor Mac Eclipse IDE for Java Developers. Apache NetBeans. MacroMates TextMate. Other text editor Linux Eclipse IDE for Java Developers. Apache NetBeans. Vim. Other text editor.",
    "Windows Eclipse IDE for Java Developers. Apache NetBeans. Microsoft XML Notepad. Other text editor. Mac Eclipse IDE for Java Developers. Apache NetBeans. MacroMates TextMate. Other text editor. Linux Eclipse IDE for Java Developers. Apache NetBeans. Vim. Other text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".WIXLIB",
    "WiX Library File",
    "3.0",
    "Library file created by WiX Toolset, a Windows installer development utility; contains a library of setup functions, including references connected to other elements in XML form. NOTE: In order to share the WIXLIB file with different WiX packages, it must be included when linking the setup package. Windows WiX ToolsetFree.",
    "Windows WiX Toolset.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".FD",
    "DataFlex Data File",
    "3.0",
    "File used by LaTeX, a typesetting system used to create formatted documents; contains a font declaration that maps LaTeX font properties to TeX font specifications; used to describe how LaTeX font attributes are translated into TeX fonts. FD files are stored in a plain text format and contain commands that declare font families, font provider files, and font encodings. Mac LyX/Mac. AUCTEX Windows LaTeXEditor Lyx. JLatexEditor AUCTEX Linux Lyx. JLatexEditor. Data file used by DataFlex, a 4GL (Fourth-Generation Programming Language) used for creating business database applications; contains references to an internal DataFlex number as well as DataFlex column definitions. FD files are used by the DataFlex compiler to associate program code references with database tables. Windows Data Access Visual DataFlex. An FD file is a disk image used by the IBochs PC Emulator for Android. It contains a copy of a floppy disks contents, which can be loaded in IBochs. IBochs comes with one included FD file, named FREEDOS.FD. IBochs is an Android port of the Bochs PC emulator. It allows users to emulate various operating systems on their Android devices. To load an operating system or other data, users can save that data as an FD file. Users can then load the data using IBochs virtual floppy disk drive. For example, IBochs uses FREEDOS.FD to load the FreeDOS operating system upon initial startup. After using an FD file to load data, users can write that data to an IBochs.HD file. NOTE: Users can rename other floppy disk image files (such as.IMG files) to use the.fd extension to use them with IBochs. You can open an FD file with IBochs PC Emulator (Android). If you are using an FD file to boot an operating system: Make sure your FD file is stored in ~/​Android/​data/​lb.myapp.lbochs/​files. Open IBochs and press F12 to access the boot menu. Select 1. Floppy. IBochs will load the operating system your FD file contains. Android IBochs PC EmulatorFree.",
    "Mac LyX/Mac. AUCTEX. Windows LaTeXEditor Lyx. JLatexEditor AUCTEX. Linux Lyx. JLatexEditor. Windows Data Access Visual DataFlex. Android IBochs PC Emulator.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".WIXMST",
    "WiX Transform File",
    "3.0",
    "Transform file created by the WiX toolset, a Windows installer development utility; produced to represent the difference between a pair outputs or XML outputs; contains the MsiFileHash table that organizes the differences; appears in XML form. NOTE: The Pyro tool included in the WiX Toolset uses the.WIXMSP file and one or more WIXMST files to produce a.MSP file. Windows WiX ToolsetFree.",
    "Windows WiX Toolset.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".PFG",
    "jEEPers Program Configuration File",
    "3.0",
    "File used by jEEPers, a program used to modify AVR microcontrollers; contains instructions for modifying on-board AVR programs; specifies a base Intel Hex-formatted microcontroller image as well as configuration changes to the image. PFG files are stored in a plain text format and contain proprietary code that is interpreted by the jEEPers scripting engine. Windows jEEPers.",
    "Windows jEEPers.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".LICX",
    "Visual Studio License File",
    "3.0",
    "Text file that identifies the licensed classes used in a Visual Studio.NET project; links the license files to the corresponding resources listed in the licenses.licx file. Resources include assemblies, controls, components, and other resource types. Visual Studio uses the License Controller (LC.exe) to link the project resources to the appropriate objects. This generates a.LICENSES file used in the assembly process. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022.",
    "Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".XOJO_MENU",
    "Xojo Menu File",
    "3.0",
    "Menu file created by Xojo, an IDE used to create custom applications for Windows, OS X, and Linux platforms; replaced the.RBM file extension; used to share a menu bar in the Xojo project (text) format; includes other menu bar information, such as menus, behaviors, items, and platform-specific shortcuts. Xojo projects can be saved in three different formats: Project (text), Binary, and XML. The selected format dictates the menu format. If you do not have a product license, you will only be able to save in the binary format, which closely resembles the old Real Studio binary format. To create a XOJO_MENU file, click the tab of the menu bar you wanted to export, select File → Export [menu bar name].., choose the save location, name the file, and click Save. To import the XOJO_MENU file, select File → Import, navigate to your file location, and click Import. NOTE: REAL Software Real Studio became Xojo, Inc. Xojo in 2013. Windows Xojo. Mac Xojo. Linux Xojo.",
    "Windows Xojo. Mac Xojo. Linux Xojo.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".XCAPPDATA",
    "Xcode App Data File",
    "3.0",
    "Data file exported from Xcode, Apples development IDE for Mac OS X and iOS; saves a package of user files that are saved by app on the iPhone; used by developers when testing apps. XCAPPDATA files can be created in Xcode using the following steps: Connect your iPhone and open Xcode.Open Xcodes Organizer window and select the Devices view. Under your iPhone device, choose Applications, and then select an app from the window. Click the Download button at the bottom of the window. You can view the contents of an XCAPPDATA file by right-clicking it and selecting Show Package Contents.. Mac Apple XcodeFree.",
    "Mac Apple Xcode.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".IWB",
    "IWBasic Source Code File",
    "3.0",
    "Source code file created by IWBasic (formerly Emergence BASIC), an IDE used to develop Windows 32-bit applications; written in the iBasic programming language, which is similar to BASIC and was developed by IonicWind Software. Even though IWBasic programs are written in iBasic, they are compiled natively for Windows and therefore are fast. iBasic supports network access, database access, 2D and 3D graphics. The language also supports both procedural and object oriented programming. Windows File Viewer Plus. IonicWind IWBasic.",
    "Windows File Viewer Plus. IonicWind IWBasic.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".MLAPP",
    "MATLAB Application",
    "3.0",
    "An MLAPP file is an application created by The MathWorks MATLAB, a programming and numeric computing program for analyzing data, developing algorithms, and building models. It stores information about the app in the proprietary MATLAB App (MLAPP) format, including the layout of its visual components, the MATLAB code configuring the apps capabilities, and metadata. Examples of apps you might create and save as MLAPP files include a data visualizer for measuring and graphing blood sample data or a tool for measuring the different energy outputs of solar panels. MLAPP file open in The MathWorks MATLAB R2023a MATLAB is technical software that features a numerical computing environment and dedicated programming language. Engineers and scientists use MATLAB in various fields, including biological sciences, robotics, machine learning, wireless communications, risk management, and signal processing. Among its many features, MATLAB provides an integrated development environment IDE that allows you to create apps. With this feature, you can design the layout and program the apps capabilities, then compile it to a web app or standalone desktop app to share with other users. How to create an MLAPP file with MATLAB To create an MLAPP file with MATLAB: Select the Apps tab. Click Design App. Choose the layout you want to use. Select Save → Save or Save As. After saving the file, you can close it and re-open it for further editing. When you finish developing the app, select Share from the Designer tab, then choose to share it as an.MLAPPINSTALL file with other MATLAB users or compile it as a web app or standalone desktop app. You can only open an MLAPP file with The MathWorks MATLAB (multiplatform). To open an MLAPP file, select the Apps tab, click Design App, then select Open. Windows The MathWorks MATLAB. Mac The MathWorks MATLAB. Linux The MathWorks MATLAB.",
    "Windows The MathWorks MATLAB. Mac The MathWorks MATLAB. Linux The MathWorks MATLAB.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".ODL",
    "Object Description Language File",
    "3.0",
    "File written in the Object Description Language (ODL), a language used in the Windows software programming context; allows developers to make their ActiveX objects visible to other vendors by exposing and publishing properties of the objects. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022. An ODL file is an event log created by the Windows or Mac version of Microsoft OneDrive. It contains a list of events that occurred while a user was using OneDrive. OneDrive uses ODL files to record actions synced to a users OneDrive account. Microsoft OneDrive is a file hosting and sharing app used by individuals, organizations, and businesses to share documents, photos, and other files with each other. While users primarily access OneDrive via a web browser, they can also download desktop versions of OneDrive, which allow them to access and edit files offline. The desktop versions of OneDrive store a record of users actions in ODL files. Microsoft can use these files to troubleshoot any synchronization issues that occur after a user edits files offline. Only a users most recent OneDrive log is stored as an ODL file. When OneDrive creates a new ODL file, it compresses the previous ODL file and renames it to use the.ODLGZ extension. ODL files are stored in the following locations by default: Windows: C:/​Users/​[USER]/​AppData/​Local/​Microsoft/​OneDrive/​logs Mac: ~/​Users/​[USER]/​Library/​Logs/​OneDrive. While ODL files are not meant to be opened, you can view the contents of your ODL files using GitHub user ydkhatris OneDrive Log ODL Reader (multiplatform). Windows Microsoft OneDrive. OneDrive Log ODL Reader. Mac Microsoft OneDrive. OneDrive Log ODL ReaderFree.",
    "Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022. Windows Microsoft OneDrive. OneDrive Log ODL Reader. Mac Microsoft OneDrive. OneDrive Log ODL Reader.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".GEMSPEC",
    "Gem Specification File",
    "3.0",
    "Developer file that specifies the attributes of a RubyGems.GEM package; saves information such as the author, a description, an example usage, and the usage license; saved in a simple plain text format. NOTE: RubyGems are used for distributing developer libraries for the Ruby programming language. Mac RubyGems. Any text editor Linux RubyGems. Any text editor.",
    "Mac RubyGems. Any text editor. Linux RubyGems. Any text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".GSZIP",
    "GameSalad Marketplace Asset File",
    "3.0",
    "File used by GameSalad Creator, a multi-platform game development program used for building iOS, Android, and HTML5 games; saves an archive of assets, such as art, music, or game development components, that can be sold and distributed on the GameSalad Marketplace; allows game developers to purchase and download assets for inclusion in their games. GSZIP files are used for downloading and importing assets into the GameSalad development software. Double-click a GSZIP file to open it in GameSalad. Windows GameSalad Creator. Mac GameSalad Creator.",
    "Windows GameSalad Creator. Mac GameSalad Creator.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".GAMEPROJ",
    "GameSalad Project File",
    "3.0",
    "Game development project created by the Mac version of GameSalad Creator, a program used for making Android, iOS, Kindle, Nook, HTML5, and Mac OS X games; saves a series of scenes, actors, and tables, as well as behaviors that define how the actors interact in each scene; also saves all assets, including sounds and images. GameSalad projects can be published to the Apple iTunes Store, the Mac App Store, the Google Play (Android) store, or to the Web. Note that Google Play publishing is only supported in the Pro version of GameSalad. Choose the Publish button in the window title bar to publish the game. Before publishing games, you can preview them using GameSalads iOS Viewer or Android Viewer. The iOS Viewer can either be run on an actual iPhone or with the iOS Simulator that is provided with Xcode. NOTE: The Windows version of GameSalad uses the.GSPROJ extension. You can transfer projects between the Mac and Windows versions of the software. Windows Scirra Construct. Mac GameSalad Creator.",
    "Windows Scirra Construct. Mac GameSalad Creator.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".JCP",
    "JCreator Project File",
    "3.0",
    "A JCP file is a Java development project created by JCreator, a Java IDE. It contains a list of references to source code files for the project, as well as project settings and any other dependencies. A JCP file can be compiled into a Java program when complete. JCP project files are saved to a directory alongside the source code and other project files. Therefore, to copy a project from one computer to another, you must copy all the other project files as well. Windows Xinox JCreator.",
    "Windows Xinox JCreator.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".NED",
    "OMNeT++ Network Description File",
    "3.0",
    "Network definition file created by OmniNET++, a software package used for building network simulators; stores the network topology structure; used for describing the logical structure of the network that will be simulated in the software. NED files do not specify the behavior of the network. Instead, they behavior is defined by a complementary C++ source code file (.CC or.CPP file). Once a NED file and the corresponding C++ behavior file are created, the network can be simulated. Network description files can be created using a plain text editor, or by using the OMNeT++ IDE, which is included with the OMNeT++ download package. NOTE: OMNeT is also referred to as omnipp, where pp stands for plus plus.. Windows OMNeT++. Mac OMNeT++. Linux OMNeT++Free.",
    "Windows OMNeT++. Mac OMNeT++. Linux OMNeT++.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".RODL",
    "RemObjects Definition Language File",
    "3.0",
    "Developer file created by RemObjects SDK, a software development application used for building middle-tier software; uses and XML format and defines the services provided by the developed software, including the API and supported data formats; used as the interface, or user specification, for how to interact with the services. When implementing the RemObjects services, the RODL files are used to auto-generate source code skeletons. Then the developer can fill in the functional code that executes the logic for the services. NOTE: The RemObjects SDK is used as an extension of common development IDEs such as Microsoft Visual Studio and Delphi. Windows RemObjects SDK.",
    "Windows RemObjects SDK.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".LXSPROJ",
    "Liquid XML Studio Project File",
    "3.0",
    "Developer project file created by Liquid XML Studio, an XML development environment; saved in an XML format and stores the project name, references to project files, and file groups; used for organizing source code for XML projects. Windows Liquid Technologies Liquid XML Studio.",
    "Windows Liquid Technologies Liquid XML Studio.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".CLIPS",
    "Coda Clips File",
    "3.0",
    "Source code file used by Coda web development software; saves a source code snippet that can be inserted into webpage code; allow reuse and sharing of commonly used code snippets and other helpful functions. CLIPS files are saved to ~/​Library/​Application Support/​Coda 2/​Clips. Mac Panic Coda 2.",
    "Mac Panic Coda 2.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".F2K",
    "Fortran Source Code File",
    "3.0",
    "An F2K file is a source code file written in the Fortran programming language. The file may be a program module or an entire program and can be compiled to generate an.EXE file. It is similar to the.F90 and.F95 free format files. Fortran is a numerical and scientific computing programming language. It was originally developed in the 1950s by IBM and has birthed successive versions such as FORTRAN 77, Fortran 90, Fortran 95, Fortran 2003, and Fortran 2008. Windows File Viewer Plus. Absoft Pro Fortran. Mac Absoft Pro Fortran.",
    "Windows File Viewer Plus. Absoft Pro Fortran. Mac Absoft Pro Fortran.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".XCODEPROJ",
    "Xcode Project",
    "3.0",
    "An XCODEPROJ file is a macOS or iOS software development project created with Apples Xcode programming software. It contains project configuration data and links to files referenced by the project. XCODEPROJ file open in Apple Xcode 9 XCODEPROJ files include links to source code files (sources), docs, resources, frameworks, libraries, and products. They also store targets, executables, find results, bookmarks, project symbols, and links to implementation files and interface builder (NIB) files. To create an XCODEPROJ file with Xcode, select File → New → Project.. After saving a project as an XCODEPROJ file you can close it, then re-open it for further editing. NOTE: When you are finished developing the project saved in the XCODEPROJ file you can build the project data into an executable app with Xcode that can be distributed. You can only open XCODEPROJ files with Apple Xcode in macOS. To open the file, select File → Open... Mac Apple XcodeFree.",
    "Mac Apple Xcode.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".SCRIPTTERMINOLOGY",
    "AppleScript Script Terminology File",
    "3.0",
    "File used by macOS applications that include AppleScript support; contains a mapping between developer-defined class names and the English (or other language) terminology that is used in the implemented AppleScript; allows AppleScript to know what the valid commands are for the developers scripts. SCRIPTTERMINOLOGY files are saved in Apples standard property list format. The script terminology files also have corresponding.SCRIPTSUITE files that define a mapping between the developers classes and underlying Apple event codes. NOTE: If you change the.scriptterminology file extension to.txt, you can view the file in a plain text editor, such as Apple TextEdit. Mac Apple Xcode. MacroMates TextMate. Apple TextEdit Included with OS. Other text editor.",
    "Mac Apple Xcode. MacroMates TextMate. Apple TextEdit Included with OS. Other text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".NEKO",
    "Neko Source Code File",
    "3.0",
    "Source code file written in Neko, a high-level dynamically typed programming language; can be compiled by the Neko compiler into bytecode that is run on the Neko virtual machine, similarly to how Java programs are compiled and then run on the Java Virtual Machine (JVM). The Neko VM is available for Mac, Windows, and Linux platforms. Therefore, programs written in Neko can be compiled and run on any of these operating systems. Windows Neko. Any text editor Mac Neko. Any text editor Linux Neko. Any text editor.",
    "Windows Neko. Any text editor. Mac Neko. Any text editor. Linux Neko. Any text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".FXL",
    "FaceFX Language File",
    "3.0",
    "File created with the CryENGINE SDK, a program toolkit used for developing 3D games; saves a library of facial expressions for a 3D character; can include talking expressions or looks of concern, happiness, anger, and others. FXL files store facial expressions for a character model (.CHR file). The FXL file to load for a given character is specified in the.CHRPARAMS file. Windows Crytek CryENGINEFree. Script written using FaceFX, an application used to build facial animations for video games; saves commands that execute application instructions, such as constructing a face graph, publishing an actor, or setting up an animation; useful for running repetitive operations. FXL files save a series of instructions that could be manually entered individually at the FaceFX command line. Windows OC3 Entertainment FaceFX.",
    "Windows Crytek CryENGINE. Windows OC3 Entertainment FaceFX.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".GNUMAKEFILE",
    "GNU Makefile",
    "3.0",
    "File used by the GNU Make utility, a tool designed to build libraries and programs by executing source code, or makefiles; contains a list of commands that direct the shell its running; other makefile extensions include.MAKEFILE and.MK; its commands are shell-specific, not universal. Windows GNU Make. Sublime Text. Mac GNU Make. Sublime Text. Linux GNU Make. Sublime Text.",
    "Windows GNU Make. Sublime Text. Mac GNU Make. Sublime Text. Linux GNU Make. Sublime Text.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".FIMPP",
    "FiM++ Source Code File",
    "3.0",
    "Source code file written in FiM++, an esoteric programming language based on the My Little Pony cartoon series; uses the form of a written letter to define classes (e.g., Dear Princess Celestia: Letter One followed by other statements); similar to other humorous programming languages such as LOLCODE (.LOL files). The FiM++ language originated from a post on the Equestria Daily by a blogger named Cereal Velocity. Windows Any text editor.",
    "Windows Any text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".P3D",
    "Panda3D Multifile ",
    "3.0",
    "Three-dimensional model created with Peak3D, a computer graphics 3d modeling program; supports both 2D and 3D images with support for polygons, cameras, and multiple types of lighting effects. Windows PeakWork Peak3D. Integral file in Panda3Ds packaging system; used to package an application for distribution; similar to.MF files but specifically intended to hold a Panda3D application instead of miscellaneous items; holds everything needed to run an application including: Python code, models, textures, and compiled.DLL files. Panda3D is a free 3D game engine used to develop and render games. Panda3D is developed by Carnegie Mellon University. Windows Panda3D. Mac Panda3D. Linux Panda3DFree. A P3D file is a 3D model used in a Radical Entertainment video game, such as The Simpsons: Hit & Run, The Simpsons: Road Rage, and Crash Tag Team Racing. It is used to show a character or object in the game. Pure3D is a game engine Radical Entertainment used to develop several popular video games. The 3D models Pure3D uses are saved as P3D files. P3D files include the meshes, textures, skeletons, and collision and physics objects that comprise a Pure3D model. P3D files are sometimes stored in.RCF files, which are Radical Entertainment game data archives. Enthusiastic players, especially those interested in modding the game The Simpsons: Hit & Run, have created tools that allow them to extract P3D files from RCF files and other game data locations. Players have also created tools that allow them to open, view, and edit P3D files. You can open a P3D file in Lucas Pure 3D Editor (Windows) or in Blender (cross-platform) using the Pure3D Editor add-on. You can also use the Pure3D.NET library to load Pure3D files. Windows Lucas Pure 3D Editor. Blender with Pure3D Editor add-on. Pure3D.NET Library. Mac Blender with Pure3D Editor add-on. Pure3D.NET Library. Linux Blender with Pure3D Editor add-on. Pure3D.NET LibraryFree.",
    "Windows PeakWork Peak3D. Windows Panda3D. Mac Panda3D. Linux Panda3D. Windows Lucas Pure 3D Editor. Blender with Pure3D Editor add-on. Pure3D.NET Library. Mac Blender with Pure3D Editor add-on. Pure3D.NET Library. Linux Blender with Pure3D Editor add-on. Pure3D.NET Library.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".XOJO_BINARY_PROJECT",
    "Xojo Binary Project File",
    "3.0",
    "Project created by Xojo, an IDE used to create desktop, web-mobile, and web applications; contains data in binary format that references the contents of the application, including menus, windows, and build settings; can be built into an executable file. Xojo projects can be saved in three different formats: Project (text), Binary, and XML. If you do not have a product license, you will only be able to save in the binary format, which closely resembles the old Real Studio binary format. To create the XOJO_BINARY_PROJECT file, select File → New Project.., name the application, and click OK. After you create the project, select File → Save As.., choose the save location, and click Save. NOTE: REAL Software Real Studio became Xojo, Inc. Xojo in 2013. Windows Xojo. Mac Xojo. Linux Xojo.",
    "Windows Xojo. Mac Xojo. Linux Xojo.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".SPL",
    "Shakespeare Programming Language Source Code",
    "3.0",
    "Animation created with FutureWave FutureSplash Animator; a precursor to Adobe Flash; compatible with Flash Player. Windows Adobe Flash Player —  (Discontinued). Adobe Flash Professional CC —  (Discontinued). Trial Mac Adobe Flash Player —  (Discontinued). Adobe Flash Professional CC —  (Discontinued). Trial. An SPL file is a file created by Windows when a user submits a print job. It contains the spooled print data, which includes the digital document contents sent to the printer for printing. SPL files save data in a raw printer format and are used as a temporary data store for the print job, which is emptied when the job completes. SPL files are saved to the following directory when a print job is submitted: C:/​Windows/​System32/​spool/​PRINTERS/​. NOTE: Windows also saves an.SHD file to the same directory as the SPL file when printing. This shadow file allows the print job to be resubmitted if it is interrupted. You can open an SPL file and view its contents in EMF Printer Spool File Viewer (Windows) and SPLViewer (Windows). You can also use these applications to print the print job described in your SPL file. Windows Microsoft Windows. EMF Printer Spool File Viewer. SPLViewerFree. An SPL file is a schematic diagram created by sPlan, a schematic editor for Windows. It contains a schematic circuit diagram, which consists of circuit components such as fuses, diodes, resistors, switches, contacts, transistors, relays, connectors, hydraulics, and capacitors. SPL file open in ABACOM sPlan 7.0 When you create a diagram and save it by selecting File → Save, sPlan creates an SPL file to store the contents of the diagram. You can also to choose to save the diagram as a template by selecting File → Template.. → Save as template. sPlan templates are also saved as SPL files. If you come across an SPL file on your computer you can use sPlan to open the file. You can also use sPlan Viewer, which is available for free. NOTE: SPL files were replaced by SPL7 files with the release of sPlan 7.0. However, SPL files can. Windows ABACOM sPlan. ABACOM sPlan ViewerFree. An SPL file is a Splunk application package used by Splunk Enterprise, a program used to search, analyze, and visualize business data. It stores all the files that make up the application, which may include scripted and modular inputs, reports, custom UI dashboards, event types, field extractions, and workflows. SPL files are used to upload apps to Splunkbase, which is the Splunk app store, to distribute them to other Splunk users. You can open SPL files using Splunk Enterprise or Splunk Cloud, which is the web version of Splunk Enterprise. Some examples of Splunk apps include User Role Checker, PyDen Manager, Slack Notification Alert, Cisco Network App for Splunk, and Palo Alto Networks App for Splunk. SPL files are the same as.TAR.GZ files but use the.spl file extension instead of the.tar.gz extension. They store the application files in TAR archives (tarballs), which are compressed with the standard GNU zip (gzip) compression algorithm. You can package your Splunk app as an SPL package by opening a command prompt and navigating to the following directory: $SPLUNK_HOME/​bin/​ Then, enter one of the following commands depending on the computer you are using: Windows - splunk package app your_app_name macOS -./splunk package app your_app_name. Windows Splunk Enterprise. Mac Splunk Enterprise. Linux Splunk Enterprise. Web Splunk Cloud. An SPL file contains a program written in the Shakespeare Programming Language (SPL). The program consists of source code that resembles the script of a play. It can be run with the shakespearelang interpreter. SPL file open in Microsoft Visual Studio Code The Shakespeare Programming Language is an esoteric programming language whose structure is modeled after the Bards plays. Each program contains characters, acts, scenes, actions, and dialogue that determines its output, by adding positive values to characters using positive nouns and adjectives and adding negative values to characters using negative nouns and adjectives. For example, an SPL file might introduce Romeo and Juliet, have them enter a scene, speak, and exit the scene. When run using an SPL interpreter, this program would likely output a simple line of text. You can open and edit an SPL file with Microsoft Visual Studio Code (multiplatform) or any other text editor. You can use the shakespearelang interpreter (multiplatform) to run the program an SPL file contains. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio Code. shakespearelang. Any text editor Mac Microsoft Visual Studio Code. shakespearelang. Any text editor Linux Microsoft Visual Studio Code. shakespearelang. Any text editor. An SPL file is a spelling dictionary used by Vim, a popular Linux text editor. It contains a language-specific dictionary used to provide spell check functionality in Vim. SPL files are stored in Vims ~/​vim/​runtime/​spell directory. Like many text editors, Vim (and its variants) contains spell check functionality that highlights users spelling mistakes. To turn on spell check, Vim users must either enter the command setlocal spell in Vims command line or add the set spell command to their.VIMRC file. By default, Vim uses English spelling dictionary files, named en.characterset.spl, to check users spelling. However, users can modify their VIMRC files set spelllang setting to use other languages spelling dictionaries. Vim contains over 100 SPL dictionaries, grouped into over 50 languages. In addition to these SPL files, Vim uses SUG and DIFF files to highlight misspelled words and show spelling suggestions. Vim SPL files are not meant to be opened. If you want to use an SPL file as your Vim spelling dictionary, you can do so by: Opening your Vim VIMRC file. Updating the set spelllang setting to use your SPL files language code. For example, if your SPL file is named fr.latin1.spl, update the setspelllang setting to read set spellang=fr If necessary, you can download SPL files (as well as each languages SUG and DIFF files) from ftp.vim.org. Windows gVim. Mac MacVim. Vim. Linux VimFree.",
    "Windows Adobe Flash Player —  (Discontinued). Adobe Flash Professional CC —  (Discontinued). Trial. Mac Adobe Flash Player —  (Discontinued). Adobe Flash Professional CC —  (Discontinued). Trial. Windows Microsoft Windows. EMF Printer Spool File Viewer. SPLViewer. Windows ABACOM sPlan. ABACOM sPlan Viewer. Windows Splunk Enterprise. Mac Splunk Enterprise. Linux Splunk Enterprise. Web Splunk Cloud. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio Code. shakespearelang. Any text editor. Mac Microsoft Visual Studio Code. shakespearelang. Any text editor. Linux Microsoft Visual Studio Code. shakespearelang. Any text editor. Windows gVim. Mac MacVim. Vim. Linux Vim.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".DPKW",
    "Delphi Package",
    "3.0",
    "File used by Delphi, an IDE used for developing Windows applications; saves a package, which can be used for extending Delphi applications (runtime packages) or the Delphi IDE itself (designtime packages); similar to a plugin or library that can be reused across development projects. Windows Embarcadero Delphi.",
    "Windows Embarcadero Delphi.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".PORTH",
    "Porth Source Code",
    "3.0",
    "A PORTH file contains source code written in the Porth programming language. The code defines a Porth module or program. You can compile PORTH files using Porth. PORTH file open in Microsoft Visual Studio Code Porth is a programming language created by streamer Tsoding. It is a concatenative, stack-oriented, and statically-typed language, based on the Forth programming language created by Charles H. Moore. Programs written using Porth are saved in PORTH files. You can compile the program a PORTH file contains using Porths com command. You can open and edit a PORTH file in any text editor or source code editor, such as Microsoft Visual Studio Code (multiplatform) or GitHub Atom (multiplatform). Some source code editors, such as Visual Studio Code, offer plug-ins that can help you more easily read and edit PORTH Files. Windows Porth. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor Mac Porth. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor Linux Porth. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor.",
    "Windows Porth. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor. Mac Porth. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor. Linux Porth. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. GitHub Atom —  (Discontinued). GNU Emacs. Any text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".TNS",
    "TI-Nspire Document",
    "3.0",
    "Calculator program created for TI-Nspire handheld graphing calculators; can include graphs, statistical data, or other application components runnable by the calculator; created by the TI-Nspire desktop software and can be uploaded to the TI-Nspire device and executed after the program is complete. TI-Nspire documents are often used by students for completing homework assignments and by teachers for teaching graphing principles. Windows Texas Instruments TI-Nspire Software. Mac Texas Instruments TI-Nspire Software.",
    "Windows Texas Instruments TI-Nspire Software. Mac Texas Instruments TI-Nspire Software.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".VC",
    "Verge Code File",
    "2.9",
    "Verge source code file; contains code written in Verge (Vecnas Extraordinary Roleplaying Game Engine); designed for programming RPGs (Role Playing Games), but can be used to create other types of games as well. Windows Verge.",
    "Windows Verge.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".DEP",
    "Windows Dependency File",
    "2.9",
    "File used by various Windows programs or Windows application components; contains run-time requirements for a program, such as needed components or the names of target installation files and directories for an installer; often used for declaring dependencies for productivity tools being integrated with Microsoft Office. DEP files can be created using the Microsoft Package and Deployment Wizard (PDW), which is part of the Microsoft Office 2000 Developer software package. Windows Microsoft Office 2000 Developer —  (Discontinued).",
    "Windows Microsoft Office 2000 Developer —  (Discontinued).",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".ENT",
    "External Entity",
    "2.9",
    "Text file containing parameters and entity declarations for a specific set of tags defined in a DTD; often referenced by XML and SGML documents. Windows SyncRO Soft oXygen XML Editor. Any text editor Mac SyncRO Soft oXygen XML Editor. Any text editor Linux SyncRO Soft oXygen XML Editor. Any text editor.",
    "Windows SyncRO Soft oXygen XML Editor. Any text editor. Mac SyncRO Soft oXygen XML Editor. Any text editor. Linux SyncRO Soft oXygen XML Editor. Any text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".PCH",
    "Precompiled Header File",
    "2.9",
    "Output file created by MSC Nastran, a structural analysis program that enables you to test the integrity of different structures such as spacecraft or buildings; contains different types of data output depending on the card (similar to a command) that is run. An example of a card is XYPUNCH, which will create an output of the X and Y-axis data, e.g., magnitude vs. frequency. The contents of the PCH file created from this card would consist of two types of data. The first type would resemble the.F06 file, with mode shapes, static displacements, and grid point stress data, but in alternate format from the F06 file. The second type of output would display magnitude vs. frequency, which helps plot the response curve in a spreadsheet. Windows MSC Nastran. Linux MSC Nastran. Contains programming code referenced by a source code file, often using an #include preprocessor directive; may reference other header (.H) files and other resources. PCH files are used by Microsoft Visual C++ and other software development programs, such as Apple Xcode. PCH files used by Xcode are called Prefix Header files and are typically named [name]_Prefix.pch.. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022. Code::Blocks. Any text editor Mac Apple Xcode. Code::Blocks. MacroMates TextMate. Any text editor Linux Code::BlocksFree.",
    "Windows MSC Nastran. Linux MSC Nastran. Windows Microsoft Visual Studio 2022. Code::Blocks. Any text editor. Mac Apple Xcode. Code::Blocks. MacroMates TextMate. Any text editor. Linux Code::Blocks.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".QML",
    "Qt Modeling Language File",
    "2.9",
    "A QML file is a source code file created with Qt SDK, a software development IDE used to create user interfaces for desktop and mobile applications. It contains QML source code encoded in UTF-8 format. QML files start with an import statement since they must import at least one imported library. Below is an example QML source code snippet: import QtQuick 1.0 Rectangle { width: 120; height: 160; resources: [ Component { id: contactDelegate Text { text: modelData.firstName +   + modelData.lastName } } ] ListView { anchors.fill: parent model: contactModel delegate: contactDelegate } }. Windows Qt SDK. Mac Qt SDK. Linux Qt SDK.",
    "Windows Qt SDK. Mac Qt SDK. Linux Qt SDK.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".SPEC",
    "RPM Specification File",
    "2.9",
    "File used by rpmbuild, a program used for building.RPM (Red Hat Package Manager) packages, which are used for installing Linux programs; includes the package name, version, copyright information, a summary, references to program files, and instructions for creating and installing the package. SPEC files can be created and edited with any text editor. Linux rpmbuild. Any text editor.",
    "Linux rpmbuild. Any text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".ATLAS",
    "Texture Atlas Skin File",
    "2.9",
    "An ATLAS file is a skin configuration file used by libGDX, an open source game development framework. It is used to configure the appearance of UI elements, or widgets, in a game, such as fonts, buttons, labels, and images. ATLAS files contain the position and size of images for the UI elements, which are packed in a.PNG file to decrease game loading time. The ATLAS file is one of 3 files that make up a texture atlas, which is used to reduce the number of separate image files used for the UI elements in the game. The following three files are required in libGDX to create a skin for UI elements in a game: PNG file - This file contains all the images used to make up the appearance of the UI elements. The images are packed in one file to reduce memory usage. ATLAS file - This file contains the properties of each image stored in the PNG file. It includes the name and size of the packed PNG file and the behavior and xy locations of each image in the packed PNG file. JSON file - This file contains information that maps the images specified in the ATLAS file to each UI element. NOTE: The ATLAS, PNG, and.JSON files that make up a texture atlas should be located in the same folder of the game assets with the same name. Windows libGDX. Any text editor Mac libGDX. Any text editor Linux libGDX. Any text editor. An ATLAS file is a settings file created by Phaser Editor, an IDE used to develop HTML5 games. It is used to generate a texture atlas, which is a.PNG image packed with a collection of sprites that are used for textures of objects and characters in a game. ATLAS files contain the settings for a texture atlas and the paths to each sprite to be packed in the PNG image when the game is built. You can open and edit ATLAS files in Phaser Editor. When you are finished editing the ATLAS file, you can generate the texture atlas. The texture atlas includes a PNG image with the sprite textures and a.JSON file, which contains the placement of each sprite in the PNG file. The JSON file is referenced by the game to apply the correct textures to each character or object in the game. Texture atlases are typically utilized to combine sprite textures for characters or objects in a game into one larger file. This feature reduces the number of separate sprite texture files. Instead of using multiple images to texture objects and characters, developers can use Phaser Editor or another framework to generate one PNG file with multiple sprites packed in it. The graphics hardware in the device running the game can treat the sprites as one unit instead of multiple units, which reduces the number of bind and draw calls and saves memory. Texture atlases may be created by a variety of 2D game frameworks, such as Photon Storm Phaser and Apple SpriteKit. Some games that use texture atlases, include Iron Armada, Surge Breaker, Goodnight, and Protolife. Although Phaser Editor uses ATLAS files to generate texture atlases, other frameworks, such as Photon Storm Phaser and Apple SpriteKit, do not use an ATLAS file. Instead, developers using these frameworks place sprites inside a folder with the.atlas extension and the framework generates the PNG texture atlas and JSON or.XML file when the game is built. Windows Phaser Editor. Any text editor Mac Phaser Editor. Any text editor Linux Phaser Editor. Any text editor.",
    "Windows libGDX. Any text editor. Mac libGDX. Any text editor. Linux libGDX. Any text editor. Windows Phaser Editor. Any text editor. Mac Phaser Editor. Any text editor. Linux Phaser Editor. Any text editor.",
    "?"],
    ["developer_files",
    ".C",
    "Lite-C Script",
    "2.9",
    "A C file is a source code file for a C or C++ program. It may include an entire programs source code or may be one of many source files referenced within a programming project. Users can edit C files with source code and plain text editors, then compile them with a C/C++ compiler for the target platform before the code can be run. C file open in Microsoft Visual Studio Code 1.33 Dennis Ritchie originally developed the C programming language to create Unix utilities in Bell Labs in the late 1960s and early 1970s. Since the 1970s, the popularity of C has grown, and many users have learned and used the language to develop operating systems, database management systems, games, and more. In addition, the American National Standards Institute (ANSI) and the International Organization for Standardization (ISO) have also standardized the C programming language since 1989. The language has also influenced many programming languages, including C++, PHP, Perl, Python, Java, and JavaScript. For example, C++ is an extension of the C programming language and was released in the mid-1980s. Developers use the language to create many of todays software programs. The C++ language may be stored in C,.CC,.CPP,.CXX, and C++ source code files. 💡 Some Unix compilers use a lowercase.c extension for C and an uppercase.C extension for C++, although this convention is not common. main.c - A source code file typically containing the main() function, or entry point, for a C program. You can open and edit C files with various source code editors, including Microsoft Visual Studio Code (multiplatform), Code Blocks (multiplatform), and Apple Xcode (macOS). These applications offer helpful features, such as syntax highlighting and auto-completion for viewing and editing C/C++ code. You can also open and edit CPP files with a plain text editor, such as Microsoft Notepad (bundled with Windows) or Apple TextEdit (bundled with macOS). However, these text editors do not provide helpful source code editing features. C/C++ compilers You can compile the C/C++ code stored in C files to code that can be run on a computer with various free and commercial compilers. Some compilers include Microsoft Visual Studio (Windows), GNU Compiler Collection (multiplatform), Eclipse IDE for C Developers (multiplatform), and Apple Xcode (macOS). Windows File Viewer Plus. Microsoft Visual Studio 2022. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Eclipse IDE for C Developers. Code::Blocks. Embarcadero C++Builder. Borland C++Builder. ES-Computing EditPlus. BloodshedSoftware Dev-C++. ZinjaI. gVim. Mac Apple Xcode. Eclipse IDE for C Developers. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Code::Blocks. MacroMates TextMate. Freescale CodeWarrior Development Tools. ZinjaI. Linux Eclipse IDE for C Developers. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Code::Blocks. ZinjaI. GNU Emacs. Vim. Android File Viewer for Android. Script written in lite-C, a programming language designed for easily developing graphics and virtual reality applications; uses a syntax similar to C/C++ and is compiled on-the-fly by lite-C. Lite-C includes an ABT (Adaptive Binary Tree) rendering engine, programmable particle effects, a body physics and collision engine, and play functions for sound, music, and movie files. Windows Conitec Datasystems lite-C Conitec Datasystems Gamestudio.",
    "Windows File Viewer Plus. Microsoft Visual Studio 2022. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Eclipse IDE for C Developers. Code::Blocks. Embarcadero C++Builder. Borland C++Builder. ES-Computing EditPlus. BloodshedSoftware Dev-C++. ZinjaI. gVim. Mac Apple Xcode. Eclipse IDE for C Developers. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Code::Blocks. MacroMates TextMate. Freescale CodeWarrior Development Tools. ZinjaI. Linux Eclipse IDE for C Developers. Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Code::Blocks. ZinjaI. GNU Emacs. Vim. Android File Viewer for Android. Windows Conitec Datasystems lite-C Conitec Datasystems Gamestudio.",
    "?"],
